# Cross-check: "`currentReturnType` inside a lambda" — P08 implementation review, §6 condition 2

Role: **independent verifier**. I did not implement anything and did not re-review the candidate.
The single question is whether the rejection condition quoted below is well-founded.

> *§6 condition 2 — `currentReturnType` is the **member's** return type, written once at `:544` and
> not rebound by `functionLiteral` `:2338-2345`. Therefore the sites the candidate repaired with it —
> `tryReturnLines` `:5296`/`:5310`, the return-position switch `:810`, the `armLines` fallback
> `:5675` — still borrow the enclosing function's unrelated return type whenever the return contract
> belongs to a lambda.*
> (`dc-warn/out/p08-implementation-review/REPORT.md`)

## Verdict

**CONFIRMED.** The mechanism is exactly as the review states, with two line-number corrections, and I
built an accepted-source counterexample that emits wrong Swift and fails `swiftc` at all four named
sites. The sites are **not** safe.

- Source: `currentReturnType` is a `SwiftExpr` **instance field** (`:218`); its only write is in
  `functionBody` (`:544`), cleared at `:563`; `functionLiteral` (`:2338-2344`) rebinds only
  `currentFuncReturnsOptional`. The lambda's real contract is `f.t`, already used at `:2349` to print
  the closure result type. All four named sites (`:810`, `:5296`, `:5310`, `:5675`) are exactly as cited.
- Construction: a new fixture (not the review's `probeE`) is **accepted** by the candidate
  (`haxe` rc=0) and then produces **7 `swiftc` errors**, of which 6 are directly the lambda borrow
  (the 7th is condition 1's `return`-stripping bug, exposed by the same shape). The pristine base
  produces 13 errors on the same fixture.
- Reachability: **live**, not latent. Ordinary Haxe: an annotated lambda or a named local function
  returning `ReadOnlyArray<T>`, body containing a try-return or a multi-statement `return switch`,
  inside a member whose return type differs.

